The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2336 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), and 2449 (2018), and its Presidential Statements of 3 August 2011, 21 March 2012, 5 April 2012, 2 October 2013, 24 April 2015, 17 August 2015, and 27 March 2018,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Syria and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Expressing grave alarm at the continuing severity of the humanitarian crisis in Syria, where over 11 million people remain in need of humanitarian assistance, and deeply concerned by the recent escalation of violence and mass displacement of civilians in north-western Syria, as well as the dire humanitarian conditions across north-eastern Syria,

Determining that the deteriorating humanitarian situation in Syria continues to constitute a threat to peace and security in the region,

Underscoring that civilians and civilian infrastructure, including hospitals and other medical facilities, schools and water facilities, must be protected in accordance with international humanitarian law, and condemning all acts of violence directed against civilians and civilian infrastructure,

Reiterating that the United Nations humanitarian agencies and their implementing partners must be enabled to deliver life-saving assistance to all people in need throughout Syria by the most direct and effective routes, including cross-border and cross-line, in accordance with the humanitarian principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence,

Taking note of the reports of the Secretary-General on the implementation of Security Council resolutions on Syria, which emphasize that cross-border humanitarian assistance remains an indispensable lifeline for millions of civilians in northern Syria,

1. Demands the full and immediate implementation of all provisions of all relevant Security Council resolutions, including resolutions 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), and 2449 (2018);

2. Calls upon all parties to ensure full, safe, immediate, unhindered and sustained humanitarian access throughout Syria, including across conflict lines, to reach all people in need;

3. Decides to renew the decisions in paragraphs 2 and 3 of Security Council resolution 2165 (2014) for a period of twelve months, that is, until 10 January 2021, with the exception of the border crossing of Al-Ramtha, thereby authorizing the use of the border crossings of Bab al-Salam, Bab al-Hawa, and Al Yarubiyah;

4. Decides further that the United Nations Monitoring Mechanism for the Syria Humanitarian Operations shall continue to monitor the humanitarian nature of consignments passing through the border crossings referred to in paragraph 3;

5. Requests the Secretary-General to conduct an independent review of the United Nations humanitarian cross-border operations within six months, including recommendations on how to further strengthen the Monitoring Mechanism and improve cross-line assistance;

6. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the implementation of this resolution, and on compliance by all relevant parties in Syria, at least every 60 days, within the framework of his reporting on the humanitarian situation in Syria;

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

